Charnel-Skank
Ingredients: BREAKBEAT GARAGE & GRIME + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Piccolo + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Its first record was cut aboard a generation ship halfway to Proxima Centauri.

Charnel-Skank is an airless, high-velocity collision of 138-BPM UK garage swing, jagged grime syncopation, and suffocating 1970s gothic horror. Piercing, shrill silver piccolos scream like hull punctures over swampy tape-saturated Mellotron choirs, funeral organ stabs, and bone-rattling 808 square-wave sub-bass, evoking the sound of Victorian specters trapped inside a nuclear-powered deep-space ark.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 138–142 BPM; frantic, skippy 2-step garage swing destabilized by sudden, predatory 8-bar grime bass drops and stuttered snare rolls.
- Harmonic Palette: Locrian and Phrygian dominant modes, minor-second dissonances, tritone organ clusters, and weeping 1970s baroque progressions dragged down into pitch-drifting decay.
- Production Signatures: 1970s reel-to-reel tape flutter pushed into heavy magnetic saturation; cavernous space-hull impulse reverbs captured in empty metal coolant reservoirs; dynamic sidechain ducking where sub-bass violently compresses shrill, flutter-tongued piccolo screeches.
- Dynamics: Suffocating, whisper-quiet tension built on dry ticking hi-hats and distant liturgical organs, erupting into unhinged, concussive bass warfare fronted by overdriven piccolo trills.

